-- chem/bareisswit.cub — the fraction-free Gram–Schmidt witness of every form, and its count of negative pivots as the
-- form's inertia. Over a type A with zero, sum, product and negation, each law a hypothesis where it is used, and a
-- form f on A^(m+1) additive and homogeneous in its first argument: with e = (1, 0), a = f(e, e) the pivot and
-- j(w) = (0, w), the step t(w) = a·j(w) + (−f(j(w), e))·e has f(t(w), e) = 0 (`bw-te`: a·h + (−h)·a = 0), with no
-- division; the reduced form g(w, w′) = f(t(w), t(w′)) on A^m is again additive and homogeneous in its first argument
-- (`bw-ga`, `bw-gs`, from −(p + q) = (−p) + (−q) and −(c·x) = c·(−x), both proved from the ring's laws). The witness is
-- e followed by t of the reduced form's witness (`bw-wit`); when every pivot met is nonzero (`bw-flag`) it is a
-- diagonalizing witness in chem/inertiathm.cub's sense (`bw-ok`), of n vectors (`bw-wlen`), and its count of negative
-- vectors is the number of negative pivots (`bw-wcount`, `bw-neg`). With Sylvester's law (`it-count-form`): for every
-- symmetric bilinear form on Aⁿ whose pivots are all nonzero, the number of negative pivots equals the count of any
-- other diagonalizing witness of n vectors (`bw-inertia-form`; `bw-inertia-matrix` for a symmetric matrix's form). The
-- pivots here are f(e, e), then the reduced forms' corners: for a matrix [[a, bᵀ], [b, C]] the reduced form is
-- a·(a·C − b bᵀ), the corner times the fraction-free Schur complement. Not here: that these pivots are positive
-- multiples of D_(k−1)·D_k for the leading minors D_k that chem/inertia.cub's elimination returns (Sylvester's
-- identity), so that the number of negative pivots is the number of sign changes along 1, D_1, …, D_n; and the laws
-- for the binary integers.
import "inertiathm.hypercubical"

